<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Numa Pompilius</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">trad. r. 715–673 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Numa Pompilius was, according to Roman tradition, the second king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, a Sabine from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cures">Cures</a> chosen after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romulus">Romulus</a>. Legend credits him with founding Rome's religious institutions, including the priesthoods, the Vestal Virgins and the religious calendar. His reign was remembered as peaceful, though his historical existence cannot be confirmed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/numa-pompilius.jpg" alt="Numa Pompilius" width="320" height="301" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Classical Numismatic Group · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Numapisocng6371obverse.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Numa Pompilius is the second king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in tradition, of Sabine origin. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> he was the youngest of the four sons of Pomponius and was born on the day of Rome's founding, traditionally 21 April 753 BCE. He lived a severe life of discipline. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/titus-tatius">Titus Tatius</a>, king of the Sabines, gave him his only daughter Tatia in marriage, and after 13 years she died, which led Numa to retire to the countryside. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a> places him at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cures">Cures</a> immediately before his election. Livy and Plutarch both mention the story that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pythagoras">Pythagoras</a> instructed him, and both discredit it as chronologically and geographically implausible.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romulus">Romulus</a> there was an interregnum of one year, in which senators exercised royal power in rotation, each for five days. In 715 BCE, after bickering between the Roman and Sabine factions, the Senate elected Numa, then about forty years old. He refused at first, arguing that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> was still a country of war and needed a leader of armies, until his father, Sabine kinsmen and two senators persuaded him. He asked that an augur consult the gods, and the omens from Jupiter were favourable.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> says his first act was to disband the personal guard of 300 celeres that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romulus">Romulus</a> had kept. He built a temple of Janus whose doors stayed shut throughout his reign, a unique case in Roman history. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a> calls Romulus a king of war and Numa a king of peace. Numa died of old age and, at his own request, was buried in a stone coffin on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janiculum">Janiculum</a> rather than cremated. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tullus-hostilius">Tullus Hostilius</a> succeeded him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Many of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>'s religious institutions are attributed to Numa, among them the Roman calendar, the Vestal Virgins, the cult of Mars, the cult of Jupiter and the office of pontifex maximus. He is said to have created the flamines of Jupiter, Mars and Quirinus and the Salii, who carry the sacred shields called Ancilia, and to have introduced the cult of Terminus, god of boundaries. By tradition he divided the year into twelve months and introduced January and February.</p><p>Legend gave him a close relationship with the nymph Egeria, who taught him to be a wise legislator. He was said to have written sacred books, buried with him. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a>, stone coffers found at the foot of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janiculum">Janiculum</a> in 181 BCE held two bundles of seven books each, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> on pontifical law and in Greek on philosophy. The praetor judged them dangerous to religion and the Senate ordered them burnt in the Comitium.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Numa Pompilius” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His Sabine successor <b>Numa Pompilius</b>, who negotiated directly with the gods, was credited with the first calendar, the priesthoods of the Salii, flamines and Vestals, the cults of Jupiter, Mars and Quirinus, and the Temple of Janus.</blockquote><a href="/religion/roman-religion">Roman religion · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Tradition credits <b>Numa Pompilius</b> with Rome's religious institutions and Servius Tullius with the census and a new army based on wealth classes.</blockquote><a href="/state/roman-kingdom">Roman Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>In Roman legend the abduction of the Sabine women led to joint rule between Romulus and the Sabine king Titus Tatius, and Rome's second king <b>Numa Pompilius</b> came from Cures.</blockquote><a href="/state/sabines">Sabines</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Cures, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.18,12.69&amp;year=-688" aria-label="Cures on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4858 663 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Numa Pompilius"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5308 933h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5308 933h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=42.18,12.69&amp;year=-688">What was here in 688 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
